If you read this file _as_is_, just ignore the funny characters you see.
It is written in the POD format (see pod/perlpod.pod) which is specially
designed to be readable as is.

=head1 NAME

perlios - Perl under iOS, Apple TV and Apple Watch

=head1 SYNOPSIS

This document briefly describes an experimental procedure to cross
compile Perl targeting iOS, tvOS and watchOS (and their simulators)
using a macOS or Mac OS X host.

=head1 BUILDING REQUISITES

In order to build your own version of Perl you will need 'make', which
is part of Apple's developer tools - also known as Xcode. From Mac OS X
10.7 "Lion" onwards, it can be downloaded separately as the 'Command
Line Tools' bundle directly from
L<https://developer.apple.com/downloads/> (you will need a free account
to log in), or as a part of the Xcode suite, freely available at the App
Store. Xcode is a pretty big app, so unless you already have it or
really want it, you are advised to get the 'Command Line Tools' bundle
separately from the link above. If you want to do it from within Xcode,
go to Xcode -> Preferences -> Downloads and select the 'Command Line
Tools' option.

This procedure assumes that you have Xcode.app in /Applications and the
SDKs in standard locations. If they are not, the build scripts might
fail.

Configure is run with C<-Dusecrosscompile>, C<-Dtargetarch>, and the
iOS-specific C<targetrun=darwin-ios> runner. Configure builds host
C<miniperl> and C<generate_uudmap> from the same source tree and uses
those host binaries during the target build. The target SDK selects
device versus simulator. Device CPU architecture comes from
C<PERL_ARCH>; simulator builds use the macOS host architecture.

The build itself needs no host Perl: Configure builds a host C<miniperl>
and C<generate_uudmap>, and the build scripts use them. The test script
still needs a host Perl 5 for packaging and test setup. Set
C<MACOS_PERL_PATH> to that interpreter if it is not the Perl found on
C<PATH>. Its version does not need to match the Perl being built. The
packaging scripts use only core Perl modules.

The framework's C<ppport.h> is generated by C<make> through C<mkppport.lst>,
like the other core extensions. Run C<ios/build.sh> (or at least C<make>)
before building the framework directly with Xcode; a clean checkout does not
contain that header.

=head1 BUILD

The following commands will try to build Perl (up to 'make test_prep'
inclusive).

It is not possible to run 'make test' on the cross compiled target. This
requires a test app running on the device or simulator to execute the
tests and report the results. Please see the section TESTING.

=head2 Locale capability on the iOS simulator

The iOS simulator can expose locale APIs and locale names without providing
the usable locale data and behavior available to Perl on the target device.
The simulator is intended to model the supported iOS runtime, so simulator
builds define C<-DNO_LOCALE> to report this effective capability consistently
with the device build. This lets Perl's existing locale capability checks skip
locale-dependent tests naturally instead of discovering names that cannot be
used at runtime.

If the build succeeds it will then execute 'make install' in
$INSTALL_DIR.

From the Perl source directory, for example:

  IOS_TARGET=iphoneos PERL_ARCH=arm64 sh ios/build.sh

=head2 System V IPC

The Apple SDKs expose System V IPC functions, but sandboxed apps cannot
use them. Configure therefore disables message queues, semaphores, and
shared memory for C<targetrun=darwin-ios>, including simulator builds.
A fresh default build consequently omits C<IPC::SysV>.

=head1 QUICK INTRO

Following are some examples. For detailed options see next section,
BUILD OPTIONS The ios/build.sh script will create and populate
directories 'bin', 'lib' and 'share' within $INSTALL_DIR, './local' by
default

After the build is completed several artifacts should be created similar
to this:

=over 2

=item 'bin' dir as perl-5.33.10-watch-armv7k-20200619-140059.bin.tar.bz2

=item 'share' dir as
perl-5.33.10-watch-armv7k-20200619-140059.share.tar.bz2

=item 'lib/perl5' as
perl-5.33.10-watch-armv7k-20200619-140059.lib.tar.bz2

=item build dir perl-5.33.10-watch-armv7k-20200619-140059.build.tar.bz2

=back

To build a release version of Perl for iOS arm64 in 'local' dir:
IOS_TARGET=iphoneos PERL_ARCH=arm64 sh -x "perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a release version of Perl for an iOS simulator in './local' dir,
select its target SDK explicitly. The build uses the host Mac architecture:
IOS_TARGET=iphonesimulator DEBUG=1 sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a debug version of Perl for iOS armv7 in './another' dir:
IOS_TARGET=iphoneos PERL_ARCH=armv7 DEBUG=1 INSTALL_DIR='another' sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a release version of Perl for Apple TV arm64: PERL_APPLETV=1
IOS_TARGET=appletvos PERL_ARCH=arm64 MIN_VERSION=9.0 sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a debug version perl for Apple TV: PERL_APPLETV=1
IOS_TARGET=appletvos PERL_ARCH=arm64 MIN_VERSION=9.0 DEBUG=1 sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a debug version of Perl for Apple TV Simulator: PERL_APPLETV=1
IOS_TARGET=appletvsimulator MIN_VERSION=9.0 DEBUG=1 sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a debug version of Perl for Apple Watch: PERL_APPLEWATCH=1
IOS_TARGET=watchos DEBUG=1 MIN_VERSION=3.0 sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

To build a debug version of Perl for Apple Watch Simulator:
IOS_TARGET=watchsimulator PERL_APPLEWATCH=1
MIN_VERSION=3.0 DEBUG=1 sh -x
"perl-5.33.10/ios/build.sh"

=head1 BUILD OPTIONS

Select the target SDK with C<IOS_TARGET>. Simulator versus device builds
are determined by this SDK, never by C<PERL_ARCH>; for example, both an
Apple silicon simulator and a physical device may use C<arm64>.

The following environment variables control or customize your build:

=over 2

=item PERL_ARCH

The architecture of the Perl binaries. Only one can be built at a time.
For device builds this selects the target CPU architecture. Simulator
builds must use the host Mac architecture; an explicit mismatch is rejected.

=item IOS_TARGET

The target SDK. Must be one of C<iphoneos>, C<iphonesimulator>,
C<appletvos>, C<appletvsimulator>, C<watchos>, or C<watchsimulator>.
This is required because architecture alone cannot distinguish a device
from a simulator.

=back

For direct Configure invocations with C<-Dtargetrun=darwin-ios>, implicit
include and library searches use the selected SDK and target compiler.
Additional target-built dependency directories can be supplied with the
standard C<-Dlocincpth='dir1 dir2'> and C<-Dloclibpth='dir1 dir2'> options,
or explicit C<-I> and C<-L> compiler/linker flags. These directories need not
be beneath the Perl installation prefix and must contain dependencies built
for the selected SDK and architecture. Deployment, install-name changes and
signing of external libraries are separate packaging steps.

The following OS ranges describe intended targets, not a guarantee of
compatibility with every listed OS and SDK. Older deployment targets require
fresh configuration and device testing; some APIs used by the port were
introduced after the earliest listed versions.

=head2 iOS (iphone, ipad):

=over 4

=item armv7 7.1.2 - 10.3.4

=item arm64 7.1.2 - current

=item x86-64 8.0.0 - current simulators

=back

=head2 tvOS 64 bit (Apple TV 4th and 5th generation):

=over 4

=item arm64 11.0.0 - current

=item x86-64 11.0.0 - current simulators

=back

=head2 watchOS:

=over 4

=item armv7k 3.0.0 - current

=item x86-64 3.0.0 - current simulators

=back

Device builds default to C<PERL_ARCH=arm64>; watchOS device builds use
C<armv7k>. Simulator builds use the host Mac architecture (C<x86_64> on
Intel, C<arm64> on Apple silicon). An explicitly requested simulator
architecture that differs from the host is rejected.

  PERL_ARCH=arm64 # for iOS or tvOS

=over 2

=item BITCODE

Build bitcode information into the binary when set to C<1>. The default
is C<0>; simulator builds do not use bitcode.

=item MIN_VERSION

Minimum OS version used to compile Perl and the iOS framework. It defaults
to C<IOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET> if set, otherwise C<12.0> for all target SDKs.
If both variables are set, they must match. Set an appropriate value for the
selected platform and SDK, for example C<MIN_VERSION=10.0> for a watchOS
simulator build. The available SDK and Xcode may impose a newer minimum.

=item DEBUG

Set to 1 if you want to produce a debug build. Debugging symbols will be
put into 'libperl.dylib.dSYM' file for source code debugging. DEBUG=1

`Debug` builds have no optimizations (-O0) and produce detached DSYM
symbol files along with the corresponding .dylib files. This allows for
example to plug the device with a running application and attach to its
process with full symbollication available in Xcode or lldb

=item INSTALL_DIR

Set to the name of the subdirectory within your working directory (the
one you are on when you call the build.sh script) where your device or
simulator perl will be installed INSTALL_DIR='local'

$INSTALL_DIR allows the build to find other libraries for the target
platform and arch if you want to build XS modules by using 'lib' and
'include' directories within

=item PERL_APPLETV

Set to 1 if you want to build for Apple TV instead of the default (iOS)
PERL_APPLETV=1

=item PERL_APPLEWATCH

Set to 1 if you want to build for Apple Watch instead of the default
(iOS) PERL_APPLEWATCH=1

=item IOS_DEVICE_SDK_PATH, IOS_SIMULATOR_SDK_PATH

=item APPLETV_DEVICE_SDK_PATH, APPLETV_SIMULATOR_SDK_PATH

=item WATCHOS_DEVICE_SDK_PATH, WATCHOS_SIMULATOR_SDK_PATH

Override the default SDK location for the corresponding device or
simulator platform. Set C<PERL_APPLETV=1> or C<PERL_APPLEWATCH=1> when
building for those platforms, in addition to selecting C<IOS_TARGET>.

=back

=head2 Extensions

It is be possible to build extensions with the build by placing the
tar.gz distributions in the ext directory.

Not all modules will work directly though, this is being investigated

All extensions that have associated *.bundle objects will need to be
installed at compile time because they need to be signed to run in the
device

It should also be possible to install pure perl modules from an app
running the embedded perl

=head2 Universal Binary support

The build script only builds one platform at a time. If you want to
produce a Universal binary, build both versions with different PERL_ARCH
flags and use the 'lipo' command to produce fat binaries from both
builds that support both arm64 and armv7. This can also be done with
.dSYM debugging symbols files.

Simulator builds use the host architecture. Apple TV device builds are
arm64 only. The build script currently selects armv7k for Apple Watch
device builds.

x86_64 targets cannot be built with Apple bitcode support

=head2 TESTING REQUIREMENTS


In addition to Xcode, the test script requires C<git>, C<rsync> and a
host Perl 5. For a physical device the default C<DEVICE_TRANSPORT> is
C<ios-deploy>, and automatic launch uses C<idevicedebug> from
libimobiledevice. They can be installed with a package manager such as
Homebrew or MacPorts. A simulator uses C<xcrun simctl> instead.

In libimobiledevice 1.4.0 builds where C<char> is signed, C<idevicedebug>
may mishandle child exit codes from 128 to 255. Verify those statuses on the
device or use a corrected debugger before relying on them.

On supported devices, C<DEVICE_TRANSPORT=devicectl> requests Xcode's
C<devicectl>; the script can fall back to C<ios-deploy>.
C<DEVICE_TRANSPORT=auto> tries C<devicectl> first and then
C<ios-deploy>. C<ifuse> is optional for manually mounting an app's
Documents directory and is not used by the test script.

=over

=item libimobiledevice - L<https://libimobiledevice.org/>

=item ifuse - L<https://github.com/libimobiledevice/ifuse/>

=back

=head2 BEFORE TESTING

=over

=item * Ensure you can build and deploy a simple application to your iOS
device and ensure that you have valid provisioning profile and signing
identity.

=item * Disable screen locking. It will take a long build time since you
start test.sh until tests actually start running in the device. If the
device is locked when the installation needs to happen, tests will fail

=item * Make sure your device is in a ventilated place. It will get hot

=item * Make sure your device is connected with USB to the building
host. This is only needed to install the application and perl build
directory to your iOS device. Once this is done the tests can be
restarted without USB connection, however it is comfortable to be able
to see the output in the building host terminal.

=item * Optionally restart the device to make sure as much resources are
available to run the test suite as possible

=back

=head2 TESTING

The default C<ios_harness> run includes the runtime tests from
C<ios/ios/CPAN/t>, copied by the build into C<ext/ios/t>. These tests are
discovered directly, even if absent from the top-level MANIFEST; tests also
listed there run only once. A missing generated test directory is an error.
Explicit test selections are not expanded.

The host-side tests under C<ios/test/> are not part of device discovery.
Run them separately on the build host, for example with
C<prove ios/test/t>. New runtime regressions must be
tracked before C<ios/test.sh> clones the source for a build.

To start the perl test suite on the device:

=over

=item 1. Copy C<ios/setup_test.sh> to a local configuration file, and
set C<HARNESS_APP_ID> and C<IOS_DEVICE_UUID> for the signed app and
physical device. For a simulator, set C<SIMULATOR_DEVICE_UUID> instead.
Set C<HARNESS_TARGET> to the appropriate SDK and, for tvOS or watchOS,
set C<PERL_APPLETV=1> or C<PERL_APPLEWATCH=1>.

=item 2. Run the Bash test script from the source directory, pointing it
at the local configuration:

  IOS_SETUP_FILE=/path/to/local-setup.sh bash ios/test.sh

C<IOS_SETUP_FILE> is sourced by the script. If it is unset, the script
looks for C<setup_test.sh> in the working directory and then under
C<ios/>. C<IOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET> controls the test build directory,
Perl compiler target and harness deployment target; it defaults to
C<MIN_VERSION> if set, otherwise C<12.0>. If both variables are set,
they must match.

=back

The script replaces its generated build and installation directories,
then uninstalls and reinstalls the selected app, clearing its data. Back
up anything needed in those locations before running it. It clones the
tracked source and applies tracked working-tree changes; untracked files
are not generally included.

Capture console output explicitly if a persistent build/test log is
needed, for example:

  set -o pipefail
  IOS_SETUP_FILE=/path/to/local-setup.sh bash ios/test.sh 2>&1 | tee build-log.txt

The Foundation runner writes C<perl-tests.txt> in its app data directory
after the run. The script does not create timestamped logs or periodically
download and tail that file.

=head3 tvOS simulator

The Apple TV runner uses the shared Foundation runner and the same
embedded Perl framework as the iOS and Watch runners. Xcode builds the
tvOS framework and app separately for the selected simulator or device.

For an Apple TV simulator, set C<HARNESS_TARGET=appletvsimulator>,
C<PERL_APPLETV=1>, C<SIMULATOR_DEVICE_UUID>, and C<HARNESS_APP_ID> in
the local setup file, then use the command above. For a connected Apple
TV, use C<HARNESS_TARGET=appletvos> and C<IOS_DEVICE_UUID>. The device
must be paired and configured for development. Simulator architecture
follows the Mac host.

For physical devices, C<AUTO_LAUNCH=0> requests a manual launch in an
interactive terminal. Simulator runs launch automatically.

=head2 TESTING PROCESS

The ios/test directory contains an objective-c Xcode project called
`harness.xcodeproj` that can be used build harness.app that runs tests
in the device.

With a debug build you can run and debug through t/harness in the iOS
device from either macOS shell with lldb or graphically with Xcode,
where you can also run Instruments.

Embedded Perl command handling recognizes the app's own executable name
and conventional `*-runner` or `*-runner-extension` names, so adding a
platform runner does not require another native alias.

`runperl` functionality is currently provided through CamelBones. The
runperl config hash used in t/harness is converted to JSON and passed as
a simple scalar to the ios XS method of CamelBones, which decodes
the string into JSON, and executes and captures the output of the test.
All perl interpreters are spawned as new threads, i.e. runperl. Perl
never runs in the main thread.

This version of CamelBones is forked from the original by Sherm Pendley
and provides iOS support.

https://github.com/jpalao/camelbones

Using CamelBones offered me some advantages but it also comes with some
trade-offs. As the build is actually performed twice. I realize this is
less than ideal but since it does not affect the test suite execution in
any way, I will procrastinate this problem away at this time

It is important to note that the usual mantra:

make; make test; make install

is inverted here to:

make; make install; make test

The binaries need to be installed in the host filesystem in order to be
linked with the harness.app.

The following steps outline the build and test process

(1) first Perl is built and installed in the host fs

(2) installed Perl is used to build and link the CamelBones.framework

(3) CPAN dir of CamelBones distribution is copied to the ext dir

(4) Perl is built and installed with CamelBones extension and XS .bundle
objects which also link to CamelBones.framework

(5) harness.app is built and it embeds perl5 installation +
CamelBones.framework. Once the libperl.dylib, and *.bundle extensions
are embedded and signed inside the app, and the app is installed, these
can be executed by perl calls. The `harness` project contains a script
that will copy and sign the needed Perl binaries from $PREFIX/lib/perl5
that are then embedded within the harness.app.

(6) The selected transport uninstalls and reinstalls the app. This clears
the existing app data.

(7) The test tree is copied into the app's data container. The default
physical-device transport uses C<ios-deploy>; C<devicectl> or C<auto>
can also be selected. Simulator runs use C<simctl>. The tvOS runner uses
its Caches directory; the other runners use Documents.

(8) Then all dynamic .bundle binaries are removed from Documents folder.
These cannot be loaded from outside the harness.app bundle where the
equivalent installed and signed copies are. Software cannot normally run
on iOS if it is not properly installed and signed. This has a number of
implications, for example it is not possible to install XS extensions
after the app is installed, only at app build time.

(9) The physical-device default launches with C<idevicedebug>. With
C<devicectl> selected, launch uses C<devicectl> and can fall back to
C<idevicedebug>. Simulators launch with C<simctl>.

(10) harness.app starts which in turn runs t/harness (this is hardcoded
in the harness application. You can easily change this to run any other
Perl script).

Capture the launch console output to retain the test report. The output
file and display behavior depend on the selected runner.

=head2 Known problems

iOS, tvOS and watchOS do not provide a general shell or subprocess
execution. With the embedded iOS framework initialized, C<system> can
dispatch Perl invocations to a separate embedded interpreter, but cannot
run arbitrary programs. In taint mode (C<-T> or C<-t>), C<system> checks
its arguments and environment before dispatch. The C<ios> module also
provides limited backtick emulation, not a general shell. In taint mode,
backticks check the command and environment and mark captured output tainted;
C<-t> warnings follow the caller's taint-warning settings.
C<exec> performs the same taint checks, then fails with C<ENOSYS>.

The perl binary that is built is of no use for Cocoa based programming
however libperl.dylib and the built modules can be embedded on an iOS or
Apple TV application and can be consumed from C/C++/Objective-C code.
Swift interfaces are planned.

On iOS the fork call (and others like execv) exist, however these will
fail at runtime. On Apple TV it seems to not exist at all, and the perl
wrappers will always fail. Windows-like fork emulation is in the
roadmap.

=head2 Cocoa

You can use Cocoa from Perl on iOS, tvOS and watchOS with CamelBones, a
framework that allows access to both Foundation and UIKit classes and
objects, so that full GUI applications can be built in Perl. CamelBones
can be found on SourceForge, at L<https://github.com/jpalao/camelbones>


